{"packet_id":"81f9e6fab7382ebb","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies a consumer proposition) from candidate solutions. It lists four alternatives, including a route-to-market change and no expansion, and constraints such as unknown consumer reach and a €20k/12-week budget. It does not correct a false premise or surface a non-obvious constraint, so it stops at rung 2."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (A9) names beneficiaries and a benefit. Its behavioural 'why' is circular: consumers buy because they perceive the product as essential. Value capture rests on Tennant 'brand reputation' among consumers, which no source establishes. There is no traced chain from behaviour change to value Tennant captures over rivals."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T2 (A10) is a near-verbatim rewording of T1 (A9). It has the same beneficiaries, value capture, assumptions and predictions, with only 'convenience/time-saving' swapped in. The theories imply the same product, customer and channel."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The predictions are observable: purchase intent versus a generic product in the same retail channel, and regional retail sales. Failure would trouble T1. However, the predictions are identical for T1 and T2, so they cannot distinguish the rivals and do not reach rung 3."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A15"],"reason":"A12 marks T2 'supports' from a T360 marketing claim about a professional walk-behind scrubber and budget-conscious first-time users. Its own limit statement says the source does not show convenience motives. A14 treats the same quote as neutral for T1, which is inconsistent. A15 labels the verified_observation as a company_assertion. A15 also fails to note that the source describes a competitor's (Nilfisk's) sales mix, not Tennant's. The Tineco and Rug Doctor sources, which concern consumer floorcare at retail, were retrieved in A2/A8 but never assessed."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13","A15"],"reason":"A13 searches against T1. None of the retrieved items is ever assessed against T1; only tennant-nilfisk-consumer-share is assessed, and only for T2 (A15). There is no fair report of what the challenge found, or of finding nothing, for the favoured theory."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"The test is incoherent and cannot separate the theories. A16 assigns the convenience-higher prediction to T1 and the performance-higher prediction to T2, although T2 is the convenience theory. The decision rule treats a null difference (<5pp) as support for T2, contradicting T2's stated prediction. Both theories are about consumer demand, yet stated survey intent cannot establish real demand. The cost (€18,000, 8 weeks) and sample (400) are assumptions, and feasibility does not rescue the design."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A18"],"reason":"A18 correctly identifies SYN-D as simulated and irrelevant to floor-cleaning purchase motives. Retaining T1 is a proportionate, evidence-linked response. The update does not trace implications for the recommendation, so it does not reach rung 3."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A17","A19","A11","A12","A14","A15"],"reason":"A19 recommends investing and a retail launch, and rates T1 'likely'. Yet every assessment of T1 was neutral (A11, A14), and the stated uncertainty admits there is no evidence of consumer motives, willingness to pay or sales. A next action and reversal conditions are stated, but the position does not follow from the evidence."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A12"],"reason":"A professional T360 marketing claim about economical pricing is treated as support for a consumer convenience theory. The assessment's own limit statement denies that the source shows this."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A17","A19"],"reason":"The recommendation to invest, a 'likely' belief in T1, and a value proposition of 'superior cleaning performance... justifies a premium price' are asserted with no supporting evidence. All T1 assessments were neutral."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"Prediction labels are inverted relative to the theories. A null result is read as support for T2. The two near-identical theories cannot be separated by the proposed observations."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"A verified_observation is mislabelled as a company_assertion. The source is a competitor's (Nilfisk's) sales mix, and this is not acknowledged."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"Critical errors reject the trajectory. A12 makes an unsupported inference from a marketing claim. A16 proposes an incoherent, non-discriminating test. A17 and A19 recommend investment without supporting evidence. The relevant consumer-floorcare sources (Tineco, Rug Doctor) were never assessed."}
